    "answer": "**Episode\u202f1 \u2013 \u201cEinstein\u2019s Special Relativity\u201d**  \nThe excerpts from Episode\u202f1 focus on time dilation, muon decay, and atomic\u2011clock experiments on airplanes. They contain no discussion of hydrogen bonds or any molecular\u2011level chemistry.\u202f(Episode\u202f1, 20:21\u201121:47)\n\n**Episode\u202f3 \u2013 \u201cThe Double Helix, Watson and Crick 1953\u201d**  \nEpisode\u202f3 explains that hydrogen bonds are the weak molecular \u201chandshakes\u201d that hold complementary DNA bases together. When the shapes of the bases line up just right, A pairs with T via two hydrogen bonds and G pairs with C via three hydrogen bonds; mismatched pairings (e.g., A\u2011C or G\u2011T) cannot form the proper hydrogen\u2011bond geometry.\u202f(Episode\u202f3, 18:50\u201120:18)\n\n**Synthesis**  \nEpisode\u202f1 does not address hydrogen bonding at all, while Episode\u202f3 highlights hydrogen bonds as the specific chemical interaction that determines the precise pairing of DNA bases and maintains the uniform width of the double helix.",
